#!lm
MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:6.6.t71-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:6.6.t96
REASONING=The main clause is "interim satis longo intervallo ... venit Heracleam": *venit* is the independent verb (intransitive), with *interim* as an adverb, *intervallo* as an ablative of time (with *longo* adjectival and *satis* an adverb modifying *longo*), and *Heracleam* as a bare accusative of place to which (relation 'accusative' into *venit*). Two coordinated *cum*-clauses precede it, joined by *et* (t84), which therefore links the two dependent verbal expressions (t83 *profectus* and t92 *decederet*), not neighbouring nouns. First *cum*-clause (t76): "cum esset cum M. Lucullo in Siciliam profectus" — *profectus ... esset* is the pluperfect subjunctive of the deponent *proficiscor*, i.e. a compound form of participle + *sum*; per the convention, the participle *profectus* anchors the verbal expression ('dependent', intransitive, 'unit verb' -> the conjunction t76) and *esset* relates to it as 'auxiliary'. The prepositional phrases *cum M. Lucullo* (t78) and *in Siciliam* (t81) are adverbial to *profectus*; *M.* is a praenomen relating to *Lucullo*. Second *cum*-clause (t85): *decederet* is dependent/intransitive, 'unit verb' -> t85; *ex ea provincia* and *cum eodem Lucullo* are adverbial prepositional phrases into *decederet*. Note on t76/t85: both temporal/causal *cum* conjunctions are taken as subordinate to the main verb *venit*; the second *cum* could conceivably be read as coordinate with the first via *et* alone (with *et* joining clauses and *cum* repeated), but either way both dependent verbs attach to *venit* through their own conjunction, which is what I have annotated.

